"""Single-file version.  Known-plaintext key recovery for class-XGAK ('letters in a class share one shuffle') via SAT.

Cipher (read-then-shuffle form, equivalent to xgak_sim's shuffle-then-read with k_a = r_c(o_a)):
    CT_t = D_{t-1}[k_{a_t}];   D_t = D_{t-1} o s_{c(a_t)}    (D_t[p] = D_{t-1}[s_c[p]])
    letters have distinct read positions k_a (unique decryption); no doubles <=> s_c^-1(k_a) not in K.
Key observation: whether CT_t == CT_{t+d} depends only on the plaintext between t and t+d.
P(t,d) := position in D_{t-1} of the card read at step t+d.  P(t,0) = k_{a_t},
P(t,d) = s_{c_t}[P(t+1,d-1)],  and CT_t == CT_{t+d}  <=>  P(t,d) == k_{a_t}.
Positions can be relabelled freely (conjugation), so WLOG k_a = a (letter index).
All messages start from the same D0, so P(1,d) values of different messages are equal iff their
CT symbols are equal (cross-message anchoring).
Encoding: s_c[p] binary (B bits) channelled to one-hot (permutation); P(t,d) binary via a MUX.
The class of each letter can be given, or left to the solver (K=2).
Run `python xgak_sat.py -h` for usage (subcommands: synth = experiments on fake messages, eyes = test a crib)."""
